<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan José Linz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–<a href="/years/2013">2013</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Juan José Linz Storch de Gracia, Juan Linz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan José Linz was a German-born Spanish sociologist and political scientist of comparative politics, best known for his work on authoritarian regimes and democratisation. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, he went with his mother to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1932 and took a doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> in 1959; he taught at Columbia from 1961 and at Yale from 1969 for the rest of his life. His study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>'s Spain classed it as an authoritarian regime, when regimes tended to be called either democratic or totalitarian. His works include Totalitarian and Authoritarian Regimes (1975), The Perils of Presidentialism (1990) and, with Alfred Stepan, Problems of Democratic Transition and Consolidation (1996).</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Linz graduated in law and political science from the Complutense University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1947 and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1950. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> he took classes with the sociologists Robert K. Merton, Paul Lazarsfeld, Robert Staughton Lynd and Kingsley Davis, worked closely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seymour-martin-lipset">Seymour Martin Lipset</a>, and wrote a dissertation of 900 pages on The Social Bases of West German Politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Between his years at Columbia and Yale he spent a short time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> helping to develop courses for the new Autonomous University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. He was a founding member of the International Sociological Association's Committee of Political Sociology, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seymour-martin-lipset">Lipset</a>, Raymond Aron, Shmuel Eisenstadt and Stein Rokkan, and its president from 1971 to 1979, and he was president of the Council for European Studies from 1973 to 1974 and of the World Association of Public Opinion Research from 1974 to 1976.</p><p>He supervised 65 dissertations, and his students included Alfred Stepan, Arturo Valenzuela and Jan T. Gross. He received the Prince of Asturias Award of Social Sciences in 1987, the Johan Skytte Prize in Political Science in 1996 and the Karl Deutsch Award in 2003. He died in New Haven, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, on 1 October 2013, aged 86.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Linz defined authoritarianism by four qualities: limited political pluralism; legitimacy based on appeals to emotion and on the regime's claim to be a necessary evil against easily recognisable problems; minimal political mobilisation, with suppression of anti-regime activities; and ill-defined executive powers. His work of 1975 set out a classification of regimes that also discussed sultanistic regimes, and offered a litmus test of conduct that puts democracy at risk, such as a refusal to disavow violence or a readiness to curtail civil liberties.</p><p>In The Breakdown of Democratic Regimes, four volumes edited with Alfred Stepan, he argued that the people in power in a democracy, not only its opponents, were decisive in its overthrow, and that breakdowns were contingent rather than inevitable. Problems of Democratic Transition and Consolidation compared thirteen countries and drew attention to stateness problems arising from nationalist conflicts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan José Linz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Until 1964 it was commonly defined as totalitarian and fascist; <b>Juan José Linz</b> then called it authoritarian, with &quot;limited political pluralism&quot;, and Payne called its first phase &quot;semi-fascist&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The political scientist <b>Juan José Linz</b>, defining authoritarian rule, named its features as power held by a single leader or a small group and exercised within ill-defined limits, limited political pluralism and limited mass mobilisation.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Juan Linz</b> argued in 1990 that parliamentary systems, which can replace a leader without a constitutional crisis, are less likely to break down than presidential ones, and Alfred Stepan and Cindy Skach found that new democracies with parliamentary constitutions survived far more often than presidential ones.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars who reject the claim, associated with the political scientist <b>Juan Linz</b>, that presidentialism itself endangers democracy argue that its record depends on its design and setting.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in New Haven, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.74,7.1&amp;year=1982" aria-label="Bonn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juan José Linz"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5383 5768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5383 5768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.74,7.1&amp;year=1982">What was here in 1982? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
